# Spring Boot Test 2.7.x — Test Slices & Integration Patterns

## 1. Test Slice Philosophy

Spring Boot test slices load **only a targeted portion** of the application context, making them much faster than `@SpringBootTest`. Each slice auto-configures only what is relevant for that layer.

| Annotation | Loads | Use For |
|---|---|---|
| `@WebMvcTest` | MVC layer only (controllers, filters, `MockMvc`) | Controller unit tests |
| `@DataMongoTest` | MongoDB layer only (repositories, `MongoTemplate`) | Repository tests |
| `@DataJpaTest` | JPA layer only (repositories, `TestEntityManager`) | JPA repository tests |
| `@SpringBootTest` | Full application context | True integration tests |

---

## 2. `@WebMvcTest` — Controller Layer Testing

`@WebMvcTest` does NOT load `@Service` or `@Repository` beans. You must provide mocks for all service dependencies using `@MockBean`.

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.web.servlet.WebMvcTest;
import org.springframework.boot.test.mock.mockito.MockBean;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MockMvc;

import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.*;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.*;

@WebMvcTest(LeadController.class)        // only loads this controller's slice
class LeadControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;             // auto-configured, no server needed

    @MockBean
    private LeadService leadService;     // replaces real bean in Spring context with a Mockito mock

    @Test
    void shouldReturnLeadById() throws Exception {
        Lead mockLead = new Lead("L001", "Solar", "OPEN");
        given(leadService.getLeadById("L001")).willReturn(mockLead);  // BDD-style stubbing

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/leads/L001")
                .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value("L001"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.status").value("OPEN"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldCreateLead() throws Exception {
        String requestBody = """
            { "title": "Solar Panel", "assignee": "John" }
            """;
        Lead created = new Lead("L002", "Solar Panel", "NEW");
        given(leadService.createLead(any())).willReturn(created);

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/leads")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content(requestBody))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value("L002"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturn404WhenLeadNotFound() throws Exception {
        given(leadService.getLeadById("MISSING"))
            .willThrow(new LeadNotFoundException("MISSING"));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/leads/MISSING"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturn400ForInvalidInput() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/leads")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{}"))                               // missing required fields
            .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());
    }
}
```

**Key points for `@WebMvcTest`:**
- `@MockBean` — registers a Mockito mock as a Spring bean in the slice context (different from `@Mock`).
- Use `given(...).willReturn(...)` (BDDMockito) or `when(...).thenReturn(...)` — both work identically.
- `MockMvc` performs real HTTP request/response processing (routing, serialization, filters) without a running server.
- Specify the controller class in `@WebMvcTest(MyController.class)` to limit the context to just that controller.

---

## 3. `@DataMongoTest` — Repository Layer Testing

`@DataMongoTest` configures an embedded MongoDB (via `de.flapdoodle.embed.mongo`), scans for `@Document` classes, and wires up Spring Data MongoDB repositories.

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.data.mongo.DataMongoTest;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.MongoTemplate;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@DataMongoTest
class LeadRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private LeadRepository leadRepository;      // real repository, wired to embedded Mongo

    @Autowired
    private MongoTemplate mongoTemplate;        // for setup/teardown operations

    @AfterEach
    void cleanUp() {
        leadRepository.deleteAll();             // isolate each test
    }

    @Test
    void shouldSaveAndFindLead() {
        Lead lead = new Lead(null, "Solar Install", "OPEN");
        Lead saved = leadRepository.save(lead);

        assertThat(saved.getId()).isNotNull();

        Lead found = leadRepository.findById(saved.getId()).orElseThrow();
        assertThat(found.getTitle()).isEqualTo("Solar Install");
        assertThat(found.getStatus()).isEqualTo("OPEN");
    }

    @Test
    void shouldFindLeadsByStatus() {
        leadRepository.saveAll(List.of(
            new Lead(null, "Lead A", "OPEN"),
            new Lead(null, "Lead B", "CLOSED"),
            new Lead(null, "Lead C", "OPEN")
        ));

        List<Lead> openLeads = leadRepository.findByStatus("OPEN");

        assertThat(openLeads).hasSize(2)
            .extracting(Lead::getTitle)
            .containsExactlyInAnyOrder("Lead A", "Lead C");
    }

    @Test
    void shouldUseMongoTemplateForComplexSetup() {
        mongoTemplate.save(new Lead(null, "Template Lead", "PENDING"), "leads");

        long count = mongoTemplate.count(new Query(), Lead.class);
        assertThat(count).isEqualTo(1);
    }
}
```

**Against a real MongoDB (skip embedded):**
```java
@DataMongoTest(excludeAutoConfiguration = EmbeddedMongoAutoConfiguration.class)
class LeadRepositoryRealMongoTest {
    // connects to MongoDB defined in application-test.properties
}
```

---

## 4. Service Layer — Pure Mockito (No Spring Context)

Service tests are the most common and should **never** load a Spring context. Pure `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)` is the right tool.

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class LeadWorkflowServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private LeadRepository leadRepository;

    @Mock
    private WorkflowEngine workflowEngine;

    @Mock
    private AuditService auditService;

    @InjectMocks
    private LeadWorkflowService leadWorkflowService;  // the service being tested

    @Captor
    private ArgumentCaptor<WorkflowEvent> eventCaptor;

    @Test
    void shouldTransitionLeadToInProgress() {
        // Arrange
        Lead lead = new Lead("L001", "Solar", "OPEN");
        when(leadRepository.findById("L001")).thenReturn(Optional.of(lead));
        when(leadRepository.save(any(Lead.class))).thenAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));

        // Act
        leadWorkflowService.startWork("L001");

        // Assert — state change
        verify(leadRepository).save(leadCaptor.capture());  // wait — use the @Captor
        // use eventCaptor for the WorkflowEngine
        verify(workflowEngine).publish(eventCaptor.capture());

        WorkflowEvent event = eventCaptor.getValue();
        assertThat(event.getLeadId()).isEqualTo("L001");
        assertThat(event.getTransition()).isEqualTo("OPEN -> IN_PROGRESS");

        // Assert — side effects
        verify(auditService, times(1)).log(eq("L001"), anyString());
    }

    @Test
    void shouldNotTransitionIfLeadAlreadyClosed() {
        Lead closedLead = new Lead("L002", "Solar", "CLOSED");
        when(leadRepository.findById("L002")).thenReturn(Optional.of(closedLead));

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> leadWorkflowService.startWork("L002"))
            .isInstanceOf(InvalidLeadStateException.class)
            .hasMessageContaining("CLOSED");

        verify(leadRepository, never()).save(any());
        verifyNoInteractions(workflowEngine);
        verifyNoInteractions(auditService);
    }
}
```

---

## 5. `@MockBean` vs `@Mock` — Critical Distinction

| | `@Mock` (Mockito) | `@MockBean` (Spring Boot Test) |
|---|---|---|
| **Context** | No Spring context needed | Requires a Spring context (`@WebMvcTest`, `@SpringBootTest`) |
| **Used with** | `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)` | `@WebMvcTest`, `@DataMongoTest`, `@SpringBootTest` |
| **Registered as** | Local field in the test class | A Spring Bean in the application context |
| **Resets between tests** | Yes (new mock per test) | Yes (reset after each test by default) |
| **Purpose** | Service / unit layer testing | Replace a Spring-managed dependency in a slice |

---

## 6. Best Practices for Test Organization

### Package structure (mirror production)
```
src/
  main/java/com/company/integration/
    controller/LeadController.java
    service/LeadService.java
    repository/LeadRepository.java
  test/java/com/company/integration/
    controller/LeadControllerTest.java     <- @WebMvcTest
    service/LeadServiceTest.java           <- @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
    repository/LeadRepositoryTest.java     <- @DataMongoTest
    integration/LeadIntegrationTest.java   <- @SpringBootTest (sparingly)
```

### Naming conventions
```java
// Method name pattern: should[ExpectedBehavior]When[StateUnderTest]
void shouldReturnLeadWhenIdIsValid()
void shouldThrowNotFoundWhenLeadDoesNotExist()
void shouldSendEmailWhenLeadIsCreated()
void shouldNotSaveWhenValidationFails()
```

### AAA structure (Arrange / Act / Assert) — always use comments
```java
@Test
void shouldCloseLeadAndNotifyAssignee() {
    // Arrange
    Lead lead = new Lead("L001", "Solar", "OPEN");
    when(leadRepository.findById("L001")).thenReturn(Optional.of(lead));

    // Act
    leadService.closeLead("L001", "Completed successfully");

    // Assert
    verify(leadRepository).save(any());
    verify(notificationService).sendEmail(eq("assignee@company.com"), anyString());
}
```

### Additional best practices
- **One logical assertion per test** — keep tests focused; use multiple `verify()` and `assertThat()` only when they test the same behavior.
- **Never use `@SpringBootTest` for unit or service layer tests** — it loads the full context and is 10-100x slower.
- **Use `verifyNoMoreInteractions()` sparingly** — it makes tests brittle; prefer explicit `verify()` calls for what matters.
- **Prefer constructor injection** in production code — `@InjectMocks` works most reliably with constructor injection, and it makes dependencies explicit.
- **Do not stub what you don't use** — `MockitoExtension` in strict mode (default in 4.x) will fail the test if there are unused stubs, catching dead test code early.
- **Isolate `@DataMongoTest` tests** — always `deleteAll()` in `@AfterEach` to prevent test pollution between methods.
- **Use `@WebMvcTest(SpecificController.class)`** — always name the controller to avoid loading all controllers in the package.

---

## Summary Cheat Sheet

```
Test Target       Annotation                         Mock Strategy
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Controller        @WebMvcTest(MyController.class)    @MockBean for services
Service           @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) @Mock + @InjectMocks
Repository (Mongo)@DataMongoTest                     Embedded Mongo (real repo)
Full flow         @SpringBootTest                    @MockBean or real beans
```

